Now, let me reconsider another subtle point: `Merge` when both are non-nil. Let me double-check my Merge implementation for correctness in the nil stats case:

```go
func (p EvalProfile) Merge(other EvalProfile) EvalProfile {
	if p == nil {
		return other
	}
	if other == nil {
		return p
	}
	merged := make(EvalProfile, len(p)+len(other))
	for path, stat := range other {
		merged[path] = copyStat(stat)
	}
	for path, stat := range p {
		if prev, ok := merged[path]; ok {
			prev.Evals += stat.Evals
			prev.Successes += stat.Successes
		} else {
			merged[path] = copyStat(stat)
		}
	}
	return merged
}
```

When summing, I'm directly using `stat.Evals` and `stat.Successes` — if stat (coming from p) is nil → panic. For safety, let me use stat.evals()/stat.successes(). copyStat handles nil (returns &RuleStat{}). Let me fix the summing part.

Actually, `prev.Evals += stat.Evals` — here `stat` is from `p`. If p contains a nil *RuleStat, `stat.Evals` will panic. Let me use `.evals()`/`.successes()`.

